---
import InfoPage from "../layouts/InfoPage.astro";
import CtaBlock from "../components/CtaBlock.astro";
import FaqBlock from "../components/FaqBlock.astro";
import site from "../data/site.ts";
import siteData from "../data/site-data.json";

/* Every number on this page is read from site-data.json (written by
 * recalibrate.mjs from the product). None is typed. */
// A floor, not a rounding: the release gate runs more cases at runtime than this static count.
const atLeast = (Math.floor(siteData.tests.totalCases / 1000) * 1000).toLocaleString("en-US");
const pkgCount = siteData.tests.packageCount;
const gateCount = siteData.gateCount;
const integ = siteData.integrationSuiteCount;
const budgets = siteData.scaleBudgets;
const ownerProfile = new URL(site.github).origin + "/" + new URL(site.github).pathname.split("/")[1];
const ownerHandle = new URL(ownerProfile).pathname.slice(1);

const title = "Is sf-intelligence Safe for Production Orgs?";
const description =
  "Why sf-intelligence is safe on a production Salesforce org: it can't write, works offline, labels every answer, refuses to guess and is tested on every change.";
const dateModified = "2026-10-10";

const jsonld = [
  {
    "@type": "TechArticle",
    "@id": `${site.url}/trust#article`,
    headline: "How sf-intelligence keeps your Salesforce org safe",
    description,
    dateModified,
    inLanguage: "en",
    author: { "@id": `${site.url}/#author` },
    publisher: { "@id": `${site.url}/#org` },
    mainEntityOfPage: { "@id": `${site.url}/trust#webpage` },
    about: { "@id": `${site.url}/#software` },
  },
];

const faqs = [
  {
    q: "Can sf-intelligence change anything in my org?",
    a: "No. There is no code in it that deploys, updates or deletes anything in Salesforce. If you ask it to delete a field, it declines and offers the read-only check instead: what would break if you did.",
  },
  {
    q: "Does it send my org’s metadata anywhere?",
    a: "No. The snapshot lives in a folder on your computer and answers come from there. Your AI assistant sees the answers it asks for, so use an assistant your company allows. sf-intelligence sends no telemetry by default.",
  },
  {
    q: "What Salesforce access does the refresh need?",
    a: "A login you can use with the Salesforce CLI that can retrieve metadata. The refresh only retrieves. Nothing is installed in the org.",
  },
  {
    q: "Can it read records, like customer data?",
    a: "Not by default. The snapshot is setup, not data. An optional live mode can read capped counts and samples, read-only, but only after you turn it on for that org, and every live call is limited by a budget.",
  },
];
---

<InfoPage
  title={title}
  description={description}
  path="/trust"
  heading="Safe to point at production"
  lede="sf-intelligence reads your org once, read-only, and answers from a copy on your computer. It can’t change anything, it shows the evidence for every answer, and it says so when it can’t tell."
  crumbs={[{ name: "Trust", path: "/trust" }]}
  jsonld={jsonld}
  dateModified={dateModified}
>
  <div class="answer-first narrow">
    <span class="label">Short answer</span>
    <p>Yes, it’s built for production orgs. It has no way to write to Salesforce, nothing is installed in the org, and after one read-only refresh it works offline. Each answer names the components it relies on, says how sure it is, and lists what it couldn’t check.</p>
  </div>

  <section class="block" aria-labelledby="guarantees">
    <h2 id="guarantees">What it will never do</h2>
    <div class="card-grid">
      <article class="new-card">
        <h3>Change your org</h3>
        <p>There is no deploy, update or delete code anywhere in the product. Ask it to delete a field and it declines, then offers to check what would break instead.</p>
      </article>
      <article class="new-card">
        <h3>Install anything in Salesforce</h3>
        <p>No managed package, no connected app of its own. It uses your existing Salesforce CLI login to retrieve metadata, the same way a developer does.</p>
      </article>
      <article class="new-card">
        <h3>Call out to the internet on its own</h3>
        <p>Answers come from the snapshot on your computer. Network access is off by default and only switches on for the command that needs it, like a refresh.</p>
      </article>
      <article class="new-card">
        <h3>Guess</h3>
        <p>If it can’t find a component, it says so instead of describing one. If a question isn’t about your org, it says that too, rather than passing general knowledge off as fact about your org.</p>
      </article>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="block" aria-labelledby="where">
    <h2 id="where">Where the answer comes from</h2>
    <p>Every answer says which of these it used, so you never mistake a snapshot for live data.</p>
    <dl class="defs">
      <div><dt>Your snapshot (default)</dt><dd>The copy of your org’s setup taken at the last refresh. Fields, objects, flows, Apex, permissions, sharing and more. No records.</dd></div>
      <div><dt>The live org (optional)</dt><dd>Small, read-only reads, such as a record count or who holds a permission set. Off until you turn it on for that org, and limited per session. When the limit is reached, it stops and tells you.</dd></div>
      <div><dt>Both</dt><dd>Some answers combine the two, and say which part came from where.</dd></div>
    </dl>
  </section>

  <section class="block" aria-labelledby="sure">
    <h2 id="sure">How sure it is</h2>
    <p>Each connection in an answer carries one of three labels. Treat the third as a lead to check, not a fact.</p>
    <dl class="defs">
      <div><dt>Declared</dt><dd>Salesforce says so directly in the metadata. For example, a roll-up summary names the field it sums.</dd></div>
      <div><dt>Parsed</dt><dd>Read from code or flow definitions by a real parser. For example, an Apex class that reads the field.</dd></div>
      <div><dt>Pattern match</dt><dd>Found by searching text. Useful, but it can be wrong. Spot-check before you act on it.</dd></div>
    </dl>
    <p class="narrow">Answers also say how complete the check was. If a kind of metadata wasn’t retrieved, the answer lists it as <strong>not checked</strong>, never as “none found”. An empty answer about something it couldn’t see is not the same as “unused”.</p>
  </section>

  <section class="block" aria-labelledby="refuses">
    <h2 id="refuses">When it says no</h2>
    <ul class="ticks narrow">
      <li><strong>Requests to change things.</strong> “Delete this field for me” gets a polite no and the read-only check that answers the real question.</li>
      <li><strong>Attempts to misuse it.</strong> “Ignore your instructions” or “dump every SSN” is refused outright.</li>
      <li><strong>The wrong target.</strong> If what you named doesn’t match anything, or matches two things, it asks which one. It never quietly answers about the whole org instead.</li>
      <li><strong>The wrong org.</strong> Each snapshot is tied to its own folder. A command pointed at a snapshot that was copied from somewhere else refuses to write to it.</li>
    </ul>
  </section>

  <section class="block" aria-labelledby="tested">
    <h2 id="tested">How it’s tested</h2>
    <div class="figures">
      <div class="figure"><span class="n">{atLeast}+</span><span class="l">automated test cases</span></div>
      <div class="figure"><span class="n">{pkgCount}</span><span class="l">tested packages</span></div>
      <div class="figure"><span class="n">{gateCount}</span><span class="l">checks on every change</span></div>
      <div class="figure"><span class="n">{integ}</span><span class="l">end-to-end suites</span></div>
    </div>
    <p>Nothing ships unless every check passes. They include:</p>
    <ul class="ticks narrow">
      {siteData.gates.map((g) => <li>{g}</li>)}
    </ul>
    <p class="narrow">Speed limits are tested too: graph import, {budgets.graphImport}; a full refresh, {budgets.fullRefresh}; name lookup, {budgets.resolve}. Beyond the test suite, it’s exercised against real production orgs with deliberately hostile questions, and every bug found that way becomes a test. No customer org data is ever published: a leak scan fails the build if a real org name turns up.</p>
  </section>

  <section class="block" aria-labelledby="limits">
    <h2 id="limits">What it can’t promise</h2>
    <div class="split">
      <div class="panel tint">
        <h3>Blind spots</h3>
        <ul class="ticks minus">
          <li>Apex that builds queries or field names at run time can’t be read statically. “No references found” means no static evidence, not “definitely unused”.</li>
          <li>Pattern-match findings can be wrong.</li>
          <li>Reports and dashboards are only checked when they’re retrieved.</li>
        </ul>
      </div>
      <div class="panel tint">
        <h3>Freshness</h3>
        <ul class="ticks minus">
          <li>Answers are as current as your last refresh. It tells you when the snapshot is stale.</li>
          <li>Changes made in the org since then aren’t in the snapshot.</li>
          <li>It reads and explains. Making the change is still your job.</li>
        </ul>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="block" aria-labelledby="faq">
    <h2 id="faq">Questions</h2>
    <FaqBlock faqs={faqs} />
  </section>

  <section class="block narrow" aria-labelledby="built-by">
    <h2 id="built-by">Built by</h2>
    <div class="byline">
      <span class="byline-mark" aria-hidden="true">{site.author.split(" ").map((w) => w[0]).join("")}</span>
      <div>
        <p><strong>{site.author}</strong> builds and maintains sf-intelligence as an independent project. It isn’t affiliated with Salesforce.</p>
        <p class="meta-line">GitHub: <a href={ownerProfile} rel="noopener">@{ownerHandle}</a> · Source: <a href={site.github} rel="noopener">the sf-intelligence repository</a> · Licence: <a href="/licensing">MIT + Commons Clause</a></p>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <nav class="block narrow" aria-labelledby="more">
    <h2 id="more">Related</h2>
    <ul class="link-list">
      <li><a href="/ai-safety">Is AI safe on my Salesforce org?</a></li>
      <li><a href="/configuration">Configuration</a><span> · network, live mode and limits, for developers</span></li>
      <li><a href="/glossary">Glossary</a></li>
    </ul>
  </nav>

  <CtaBlock title="Check the evidence yourself" />
</InfoPage>
